{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 1|# Franken FDK AAC \u8212- a laboratory/"geek" AAC encoder (FDK) 2| 3|Built on top of {\b libfdk-aac 2.0.3} (mstorsjo/fdk-aac) + the 4|{\b nu774/fdkaac 1.0.2} frontend, with bolted-on CLI switches that expose 5|the normally hardcoded, internal decisions of the FDK encoder. It serves 6|extreme debugging and experimentation with AAC/HE-AAC/HE-AAC v2. 7| 8|Binaries (static, no external DLLs): 9|- {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe} \u8212- Windows 64-bit (PE32+) 10|- {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x86.exe} \u8212- Windows 32-bit (PE32) 11| 12|## Download 13| 14|Prebuilt Windows binaries are published as GitHub Releases, built and hosted by 15|GitHub (no login needed to download): 16| 17|{\b \u8594 ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest
}}}
} 18| 19|Grab {\f1 franken-fdk-aac-x64-vX.Y.Z.zip} (64-bit) or the {\f1 x86} one; each zip holds 20|the {\f1 .exe} plus the full documentation. There are no binaries committed to the 21|repo \u8212- see "Build" at the bottom for building it yourself. 22| 23| 24|All original nu774 frontend options ({\f1 -p/--profile}, {\f1 -b/--bitrate}, 25|{\f1 -m/--bitrate-mode}, {\f1 -w/--bandwidth}, {\f1 -a/--afterburner}, {\f1 -s/--sbr-ratio}, 26|{\f1 -f/--transport-format}, tagging, etc.) work as before. Below are only the 27|NEW switches. See also {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe --help}. 28| 29|NOTE: this is an "I-know-what-I'm-doing" tool. Most of these parameters 30|deliberately let you go beyond what the FDK automation does \u8212- you can 31|knowingly wreck the stereo image, the bandwidth, or the quality with them. 32|The sentinel {\f1 -1} (or {\f1 0} for {\f1 --core-cutoff}) = "leave FDK's default behavior". 33| 34|Author: Micha\u322 ? Dziwisz. Subject-matter consultant: Patryk Faliszewski. 35|Built on open source: libfdk-aac (Fraunhofer IIS) + the nu774/fdkaac frontend. 36| 37|--- 38| 39|## Option groups overview (grouped the same way in {\f1 --help}) 40| 41|The options are ordered by topic, roughly from easiest to most geeky. The same 42|order applies in {\f1 --help} (groups A\u8211-E) and in the sections below: 43| 44|- {\b A. Start here (consumer):} {\f1 --verbose}, {\f1 --is-aggression}, {\f1 --speech}, 45| {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth}, {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} \u8212- sections 0, 1, 2, 10. 46|- {\b B. Stereo:} MS ({\f1 --msmask}, {\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi}, {\f1 --ms-bias}, 47| {\f1 --ms-precision}), IS ({\f1 --is}, {\f1 --isbands}, {\f1 --is-*}), PS ({\f1 --ps}, {\f1 --ps-iid-quant}, 48| {\f1 --ps-icc}, {\f1 --ps-icc-mode}) \u8212- sections 1, 4, 8. 49|- {\b C. Bandwidth and SBR:} {\f1 --core-cutoff}, {\f1 --sbr-*} \u8212- sections 2, 3. 50|- {\b D. Masking / noise / detail:} {\f1 --ath-scale}, {\f1 --spread-mask}, {\f1 --tns-*}, 51| {\f1 --pns}, {\f1 --pns-start}, {\f1 --force-pns} \u8212- sections 5, 6. 52|- {\b E. Blocks and bitrate:} {\f1 --block-bias}, {\f1 --vbr-reservoir}, {\f1 --peak-bitrate}, 53| {\f1 --max-bits-frame}, {\f1 --min-bits-frame}, {\f1 --bitres-mode} \u8212- sections 7, 9. 54|- {\b F. DAB+ digital radio:} {\f1 --dab}, {\f1 --dab-label} \u8212- section 14. 55| 56|Tip: at the end, {\f1 --verbose} prints a "franken overrides applied" section \u8212- 57|exactly those switches which, in a given run, deviate from pure FDK. 58| 59|--- 60| 61|## 0. Diagnostics 62| 63|### {\f1 --verbose} 64|Before encoding, it dumps to stderr the REAL parameters chosen by the encoder 65|(not just your overrides): AOT, bitrate/mode, samplerate, channel-mode, the 66|EFFECTIVE core cutoff in Hz, afterburner, transport, signaling, plus the state 67|of the coding tools chosen by the encoder (TNS on/off, PNS on/off, Intensity 68|stereo on/off, MS stereo on/off). When SBR is active: sbr-ratio + effective 69|start/stop freq index, freq scale, noise bands, amp res. At the end, a list of 70|your overrides (-1/0 = not set, left to the encoder). Perfect for learning the 71|starting point (e.g. the default HE-AAC v2 48k cutoff = 8613 Hz). 72| 73|--- 74| 75|## 1. Joint stereo \u8212- MS / IS / independent stereo 76| 77|By default FDK itself decides per-band about MS (mid/side) and IS (intensity). 78|Here you can override these decisions and force extreme configurations. 79| 80|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 81||---|---|---|---| 82|| {\f1 --msmask <n>} | -1 auto, 0 off, 1 on | -1 | Force MS: {\f1 0} = all bands L/R (completely independent stereo), {\f1 1} = MS on all bands. | 83|| {\f1 --msbands <n>} | -1 no limit, 0..N | -1 | Maximum SFB number that may use MS (takes the N LOWEST bands). Above that \u8212- MS disabled. | 84|| {\f1 --msbands-lo <n>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | START (lowest SFB band number) of the range in which MS is allowed. | 85|| {\f1 --msbands-hi <n>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | END (highest SFB band number) of the MS range. Used together with {\f1 --msbands-lo} as a FROM\u8211-TO pair. Bands outside this range go pure L/R. | 86|| {\f1 --ms-precision <n>} | 256..no limit (Q8) | -1 (off) | {\i (Very experimental \u8212- you probably want {\f1 --side-bias} instead.)} Scales the precision of MS bands globally (both mid and side together), LAME {\f1 -q} style. 256=no change, 384~1.5x, 512~2x. In practice its reach is limited: above ~600-800 the threshold hits FDK's hard floor and under CBR bits are only shifted between bands, so the sound stops changing. Superseded for stereo tuning by {\f1 --side-bias}/{\f1 --side-knee}, which act per-channel exactly where it matters. | 87|| {\f1 --mid-bias <n>} | 256..no limit (Q8) | -1 (off) | {\i (Very experimental \u8212- rarely needed.)} {\f1 >256} RAISES the mid (L+R) threshold after the MS butterfly to free bits from mid for side. The cleaner, better-measured way to shift the mid\u8596 ?side balance is {\f1 --side-bias} (which pulls from the same budget from the side end). Kept for completeness. 256=off. | 88|| {\f1 --side-bias <dB>} | -24.0 .. +50.0 | 0 (off) | {\b The main stereo-quality knob.} Shifts the SIDE (L\u8722 ?R) channel's masking threshold on MS-coded bands, at the exact point where FDK decides whether a scalefactor band is coded or dropped ({\f1 energy > threshold} in {\f1 sf_estim.cpp}). Sign = EFFECT: {\b {\f1 +} steers MORE bits to the side channel} (lower threshold \u8594 ? fewer side bands zeroed, the survivors quantized finer \u8594 ? cleaner stereo width, reverb tails, ambience), at the mid channel's expense; {\b {\f1 \u8722 ?} deliberately DEGRADES the side} (raises the threshold \u8594 ? side bands drop out \u8594 ? narrower, more mono image). This is the very same energy-vs-threshold decision LAME rides for its bit allocation and MusePack drives with {\f1 --ms} \u8212- nothing exotic. Because it is a fixed-budget tradeoff, at low bitrate the mid channel audibly gives up bits; that is expected, not a bug. Sane range {\b +3 .. +9} for "more spacious", negative only for extreme/artistic low-bitrate mangling. Measured on real material at 96 kbps: {\f1 +9} lifts side error in the 4\u8211-8 kHz region while mid degrades a few dB. 0 = off (bit-identical to stock). | 89|| {\f1 --side-knee <dB>} | -24.0 .. +50.0 | 0 (off) | Shapes HOW SHARPLY a side band flips between "coded" and "zeroed" at the threshold. Stock FDK is a hard cliff: the instant {\f1 energy \u8804 ? threshold} the whole band is dropped to zero. {\b {\f1 +} = SOFT knee}: bands sitting up to N dB {\i below} the threshold are still kept (coded at the coarsest scalefactor) instead of dropped, so the side fades out gradually rather than switching off \u8212- smoother decay of reverb/air. {\b {\f1 \u8722 ?} = HARD knee}: bands that only just clear the threshold (within N dB {\i above} it) are forced to zero anyway, cutting the side off early \u8212- leaner, more aggressive. Ortogonal to {\f1 --side-bias} and combines with it. Sane range {\b +3 .. +6}. 0 = off. | 90|| {\f1 --mask-slope <dB>} | -24.0 .. +50.0 | 0 (off) | Global (mid {\b and} side) tuning of FDK's {\b Masking-Slope-Adaptation} \u8212- a NON-masking heuristic ({\f1 adj_thr.cpp}) that relaxes the required SNR for scalefactor bands whose energy sits far below the frame average (stock: more than ~10 dB below), i.e. it deliberately starves very quiet bands to save bits. This knob shifts that "how far below average before I stop caring" threshold. {\b {\f1 +} raises it \u8594 ? fewer quiet bands starved \u8594 ? more detail in quiet passages, reverb tails, decays} (costs bits); {\b {\f1 \u8722 ?} lowers it \u8594 ? quiet bands starved harder \u8594 ? leaner, hollower, more bits for the loud stuff}. Same family as {\f1 --side-bias} but applied to both channels and keyed on energy-vs-average rather than the MS threshold. Subtle on dense material (it only touches the quietest bands); most audible on sparse/reverberant content. Sane range {\b \u177 ?6 .. \u177 ?12}. 0 = off. | 91|| {\f1 --is <n>} | -1 auto, 0 off, 1 on | -1 | Intensity stereo globally on/off. | 92|| {\f1 --isbands <n>} | -1 no limit, 0..N | -1 | Maximum number of SFBs that may use intensity. Above that \u8212- coded normally. | 93|| {\f1 --is-aggression <0..100>} | 0..100 | -1 (off) | CONSUMER slider: how hard the encoder should push intensity stereo. Start here, leave the advanced {\f1 --is-*} alone. | 94|| {\f1 --is-min-sfbs <n>} | -1 def(6), 0..N | -1 | (advanced) Min. number of contiguous SFBs before IS turns on. | 95|| {\f1 --is-corr-thresh <n>} | -1 def(243), Q8 | -1 | (advanced) L/R correlation threshold for IS in Q8 (256=1.0). | 96|| {\f1 --is-lr-ratio <n>} | -1 def(179), Q8 | -1 | (advanced) L/R energy balance threshold for IS in Q8 (256=1.0). | 97|| {\f1 --is-lo <sfb>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | Allow intensity stereo ONLY from this SFB upward. Bands below stay pure L/R. Only RESTRICTS where FDK may place IS \u8212- never forces it on. | 98|| {\f1 --is-hi <sfb>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | Allow IS only up to this SFB (inclusive). Pair with {\f1 --is-lo} as a range. TIP: IS usually lands on LOW bands at low bitrate, so scan small values to see the effect. | 99|| {\f1 --is-force-lo <sfb>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | FORCE intensity stereo from this SFB, bypassing the correlation / min-sfbs / loudness gates. Laboratory mode: can deliberately wreck the stereo image (IS is lossy + directional \u8212- the right channel is zeroed, only a panning coefficient survives). The stream stays legal. | 100|| {\f1 --is-force-hi <sfb>} | -1 off, 0..N | -1 | Upper SFB of the forced-IS range (inclusive). | 101| 102|### Intensity stereo in practice (how to use it, not the formulas) 103| 104|What it is: intensity stereo (IS) in the upper bands drops separate L/R and sends 105|ONE energy envelope + direction (panning) information. The ear localizes high 106|tones poorly, so this saves quite a few bits \u8212- but at the cost of stereo 107|separation (the width of the scene at the top of the band narrows). You pay for it 108|especially on material with a real L/R difference in the highs (cymbals on one 109|side, spatial effects). 110| 111|By default FDK is very CAUTIOUS with IS (hence your observation "you can barely 112|hear the difference"). There are three reasons, and that is what these knobs are 113|for: 114| 115|1. IS admission gate: FDK considers IS at all only when {\f1 bitrate/band < 5}. At 116| higher bitrates IS is disallowed entirely. {\f1 --is-aggression >=1} removes this 117| gate. 118|2. Correlation threshold ({\f1 --is-corr-thresh}, Q8, 256=1.0, default 243 ~= 0.95): 119| both channels must be at least ~95% similar to each other in a given band for 120| IS to turn on. That is very high. Lower it -> IS catches more often, even when 121| the channels are less similar. E.g. 180 (~0.70) = much more aggressive. Too low 122| = audible direction errors. 123|3. Min. region length ({\f1 --is-min-sfbs}, default 6): IS turns on only on a band of 124| at least 6 consecutive SFBs. Lower it to 1-2 -> IS also catches short 125| fragments. 126| 127|The relationship between them: for a given SFB to go into IS, ALL conditions 128|MUST be met at once \u8212- the admission gate AND correlation above the threshold AND 129|a sufficiently long region AND a stable direction. That is why lowering just one 130|threshold often does nothing (another still blocks it) \u8212- and that is why you 131|usually see no difference by manipulating correlation alone. {\f1 --is-aggression} 132|moves ALL of them at once, coherently. 133| 134|How to set it: 135|- Simplest: {\f1 --is 1 --is-aggression 40} and listen. Too little IS -> raise to 70, 136| 100. Too much (the scene "glues together" at the top, direction artifacts) -> 137| go down. 138|- 0 = FDK default (practically IS is barely active at typical bitrates). 139|- 100 = maximum: gate removed, correlation loose (~0.475), region from 1 SFB, 140| wide direction tolerance. Many bands in IS, strongly audible, saves bits. 141|- Manual tuning only when you want precision: set {\f1 --is-aggression 0} and turn 142| {\f1 --is-corr-thresh} (the main one), then {\f1 --is-min-sfbs}, finally {\f1 --is-lr-ratio}. 143| The --is-* values OVERRIDE what the aggression slider set. 144|- Diagnostics: {\f1 --verbose} shows the effective thresholds (IS corr threshold Q8, 145| min SFBs), so you see what actually went into the encoder. 146| 147|MS/IS bias (point 2): the above {\f1 --is-*} control WHEN the encoder chooses 148|intensity stereo (decision thresholds from the FDK tuning table), independently 149|of the hard on/off. {\f1 --msbands} limits MS to the lower bands (correctly \u8212- the 150|mask and the L/R->M/S butterfly are synchronized, no "left=center, right=rest" 151|artifact). 152|- Completely independent stereo: {\f1 --msmask 0 --is 0}. 153|- "Laboratory" restriction of MS to the lower bands: e.g. {\f1 --msbands 6}. 154|- Forced full MS: {\f1 --msmask 1}. 155|- More eager IS: lower {\f1 --is-corr-thresh} (e.g. 150) and/or {\f1 --is-min-sfbs}. 156| 157|### MS band range: --msbands, --msbands-lo, --msbands-hi (IMPORTANT, often confused) 158| 159|The spectrum bands are numbered FROM THE BOTTOM: band 0 = lowest frequencies 160|(bass), the higher the number, the higher in the spectrum. In a typical LC stereo 161|there are about 49 of them. 162| 163|There are TWO independent ways to limit where MS is applied: 164| 165|1. {\f1 --msbands <n>} \u8212- "the lower N bands". MS allowed ONLY in bands 0..(n-1), 166| i.e. from the bass upward to number n. This is always counted FROM THE BOTTOM. 167| Example: {\f1 --msbands 6} = MS only on the 6 lowest bands, the rest pure L/R. 168| 169|2. {\f1 --msbands-lo <lo>} + {\f1 --msbands-hi <hi>} \u8212- "FROM-TO range". MS allowed ONLY 170| in bands numbered from {\f1 lo} to {\f1 hi} inclusive. Outside that range, pure L/R. 171| It is a pair \u8212- you provide both. It lets you place MS ANYWHERE, including at 172| the very top. 173| 174|A concrete example (assuming ~49 bands in LC): 175|- You want MS ONLY on the 5 HIGHEST bands (e.g. to merge noise at the top and 176| leave the bottom in full independent stereo)? The highest bands are numbers 177| 44..48: {\f1 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48}. 178|- You want MS only in the MIDDLE of the band (e.g. 10..30)? {\f1 --msbands-lo 10 --msbands-hi 30}. 179|- You want MS on the 6 LOWEST? Simpler {\f1 --msbands 6} (or {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 180|  --msbands-hi 5} \u8212- the same). 181| 182|Mnemonic: {\f1 --msbands} = "from the bottom up to", {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} = "from..to". 183|How many bands you actually have for a given mode/samplerate is shown by 184|{\f1 --verbose} (the "active SFBs" field). 185| 186|## 2. Cutoff of the AAC core when SBR is active 187| 188|The standard {\f1 -w/--bandwidth} in FDK is IGNORED when SBR is active 189|(HE-AAC v1/v2) \u8212- because {\f1 sbrEncoder_Init()} overrides the bandwidth with a value 190|from the SBR table. 191| 192|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 193||---|---|---|---| 194|| {\f1 --core-cutoff <hz>} | 0 = default, >0 = Hz | 0 | Forces the AAC core bandwidth IN Hz even under SBR. Resistant to being overridden by SBR. | 195| 196|Example (your case \u8212- 7.5 kHz of core at HE-AAC v2 48 kbps, where the table gives 197|less): 198|{\f1  199|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --core-cutoff 7500 -o out.m4a in.wav 200|} 201|Verified: {\f1 --core-cutoff 7500} -> effective bandwidth 7500 Hz; stock {\f1 -w 7500} 202|under SBR stays 8613 Hz (ignored). 203| 204|NOTE: you police the core limits yourself. The max is the core Nyquist ({\f1 sr/2}), 205|and with {\b dual-rate SBR the target samplerate is divided by 2} \u8212- keep that in 206|mind when choosing values. 207| 208|## 3. Density / precision of SBR data 209| 210|Overrides the settings from the SBR tuning table (after it is loaded). 211| 212|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 213||---|---|---|---| 214|| {\f1 --sbr-start <n>} | -1 def, 0..15 | -1 | {\f1 bs_start_freq} index (start of the SBR band). | 215|| {\f1 --sbr-stop <n>} | -1 def, 0..13 | -1 | {\f1 bs_stop_freq} index (end of the SBR band). | 216|| {\f1 --sbr-freqscale <n>} | -1 def, 0..3 | -1 | Frequency grouping (0 = linear, higher = finer log). | 217|| {\f1 --sbr-alterscale <n>} | -1 def, 0/1 | -1 | Alternative scale resolution. | 218|| {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands <n>} | -1 def, 1..5 | -1 | Number of SBR noise bands (density of the noise description). | 219|| {\f1 --sbr-amp-res <n>} | -1 def, 0/1 | -1 | Envelope amplitude resolution: 0 = 1.5 dB, 1 = 3.0 dB. | 220|| {\f1 --sbr-data-extra <n>} | -1 def, 0/1 | -1 | Write extra SBR header data. | 221|| {\f1 --sbr-num-env <1\\|2\\|4>} | -1 off | -1 | Number of envelopes per frame. FORCES a static time grid (ignores the transient detector). More = better temporal resolution of the upper band, worse on attacks. (8 exceeds the standard grid \u8212- rejected.) | 222|| {\f1 --sbr-freqres-fixfix <0\\|1>} | -1 off | -1 | Frequency resolution of the FIXFIX envelope (0 low, 1 high). | 223|| {\f1 --sbr-stereo-mode <0..3>} | -1 off | -1 | SBR stereo mode: 0 mono, 1 LR (full separation of the upper band), 2 coupling (economical, shared envelope + level), 3 switch-LRC (by default the coder chooses per-frame). Force 1 for max separation, 2 for economy. | 224|| {\f1 --sbr-invf <0..3>} | -1 auto | -1 | Force SBR inverse filtering: 0 off, 1 low, 2 mid, 3 high. Normally driven by the tonality estimator. Higher = stronger "whitening" of tonal SBR (less metallicness at the cost of detail). | 225|| {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset <n>} | -128 off | -128 | SBR noise floor offset (small integer). Larger = more filling noise in the SBR reconstruction. | 226|| {\f1 --sbr-header-period <n>} | -1 off, >=1 | -1 | Frames between SBR headers = how fast the SBR high band "kicks in" when a decoder tunes into a live HE-AAC stream (Icecast/Shoutcast). The SBR CONFIG lives in a periodic header, not in every frame; a decoder joining mid-stream plays core-only (muffled) until the next header arrives. {\f1 1} = header in every frame \u8594 ? near-instant SBR lock (~23 ms); higher = longer core-only moment. FDK default is ~10 frames (~0.23 s HE dual-rate / ~0.46 s LC). FDK caps this to at most once per second, so very large values are clamped (e.g. 40 \u8594 ? 21 frames @44.1k). See {\f1 --verbose} for the effective period in ms. | 227| 228|NOTE: {\f1 --sbr-start}/{\f1 --sbr-stop} are validated BY FDK \u8212- an incorrect start/stop 229|COMBINATION (wrong number of master bands) will give "encoder initialization 230|failed". This is a limitation of SBR itself, not a bug. Choose pairs (e.g. for 231|64k stereo start=5 stop=9, start=8 stop=14 work). 232| 233|## 4. Parametric Stereo (HE-AAC v2) 234| 235|PS describes stereo with a few parameters (IID/ICC...). Here you can control them, 236|even at the cost of the stereo image. 237| 238|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 239||---|---|---|---| 240|| {\f1 --ps <n>} | -1 auto, 0 off, 1 on | -1 | Force sending the PS IID parameter. {\f1 0} = never (flattens the stereo image), {\f1 1} = always. Overrides the loudness-difference heuristic. | 241|| {\f1 --ps-iid-quant <n>} | -1 def, 0 coarse, 1 fine | -1 | IID quantization grid: coarse vs. fine. | 242|| {\f1 --ps-icc <n>} | -1 auto, 0 off, 1 on | -1 | Force ICC (Interchannel Coherence \u8212- channel similarity/coherence) on/off. | 243|| {\f1 --ps-icc-mode <n>} | -1 def, 0/1 | -1 | ICC rotation mode: 0 = ROT_A, 1 = ROT_B. Signalling only \u8212- the same matrix, derived differently by the decoder, so treat this as a compatibility knob rather than a quality one. | 244|| {\f1 --ps-bands <n>} | 10 or 20 | -1 (bitrate table) | Number of PS stereo bands = {\b frequency} resolution of the stereo parameters. Stock FDK derives this from the bitrate alone, so at 22 kbps and above you always get 20 and can never audition 10. Fewer bands = coarser stereo image, fewer parameter bits. | 245|| {\f1 --ps-env <n>} | 1, 2 or 4 | -1 (bitrate table) | PS parameter envelopes per frame = {\b time} resolution of the stereo parameters. Above 36 kbps stock FDK always picks 4. More envelopes track a moving panorama and transients more closely. | 246|| {\f1 --ps-env-reduce <n>} | 0, 1 | -1 (on) | {\f1 0} disables the automatic envelope-halving loop ({\f1 envelopeReducible}). By default FDK keeps collapsing 4 envelopes to 2 to 1 whenever neighbouring envelopes look similar by a hardcoded error threshold, so the envelope count you configured is often {\i not} what gets transmitted. {\f1 0} makes {\f1 --ps-env} literal. | 247|| {\f1 --ps-noenv-skip <n>} | 0, 1 | -1 (on) | {\f1 0} forbids parameter-less PS frames. By default FDK may emit up to 10 consecutive frames carrying {\b no} stereo parameters at all when successive IID/ICC sets look similar, which can be heard as the stereo image briefly collapsing and snapping back. {\f1 0} = always send parameters. | 248| 249|NOTE about PS resolution: {\f1 --ps-bands} and {\f1 --ps-env} are the two axes that 250|change {\i how many} stereo parameters are actually transmitted \u8212- in frequency and 251|in time respectively. That makes them considerably more audible than 252|{\f1 --ps-icc-mode}, which only changes how the same matrix is signalled. Measured on 253|a 4-second stereo probe with a deliberately moving panorama (0.25 Hz) plus 254|alternating L/R transients, encoded as HE-AAC v2 at 48 kbps, comparing the 255|panorama trajectory of the decoded file against the source: 256| 257|| Setting | Panorama error (RMS) | Correlation with source | 258||---|---|---| 259|| stock (20 bands / 4 envelopes) | 0.177 | 0.9655 | 260|| {\f1 --ps-env 2 --ps-env-reduce 0} | 0.138 | 0.9896 | 261|| {\f1 --ps-env 4 --ps-env-reduce 0} | {\b 0.117} | {\b 0.9944} | 262| 263|The interesting result is that requesting 4 envelopes alone changes nothing \u8212- 264|stock output and {\f1 --ps-env 4} are byte-identical, because the automatic halving 265|loop immediately collapses them again. The gain only appears once 266|{\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} stops that loop: a 34 % reduction in panorama error for 267|roughly the same file size. If you only take one thing from this group, take 268|{\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0}. 269| 270|NOTE about IPD/OPD: FDK codes IID (loudness differences) and ICC (coherence) 271|only. The interchannel {\i phase} parameters are not emitted \u8212- {\f1 ps_encode.cpp} 272|literally writes zeros and comments {\f1 "IPD OPD not supported right now"}. Note 273|however that the encoder already computes both the real and the imaginary part of 274|the L/R cross-spectrum ({\f1 pwrCr} / {\f1 pwrCi}) and currently uses only their 275|magnitude, so the phase information is present but discarded; the Huffman tables 276|and bitstream writers for IPD/OPD also already exist in {\f1 ps_bitenc.cpp}. 277| 278|## 5. Noise substitution/shaping \u8212- TNS / PNS / afterburner 279| 280|What, at medium bitrates, is replaced by noise or resynthesized. 281| 282|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 283||---|---|---|---| 284|| {\f1 --tns-mask <n>} | -1 def (0xF), 0..15 | -1 | TNS enable mask (bitwise, per block type). | 285|| {\f1 --tns-order <n>} | -1 def, 1..12 | -1 | Max. TNS filter order (short blocks additionally capped to 5). | 286|| {\f1 --pns <n>} | -1 def, 0/1 | -1 | Perceptual Noise Substitution on/off. NOTE: FDK forces PNS=off when SBR or VBR is active. | 287|| {\f1 --pns-start <hz>} | -1 def, Hz | -1 | PNS start frequency. Lower = more of the spectrum replaced by noise. | 288|| {\f1 --force-pns} | flag | off | Bypass the low-bitrate gate for PNS. | 289|| {\f1 --pns-gain <x>} | >=0.0 | -1 (off) | Loudness of the fabricated PNS noise. {\f1 1.0} = unchanged (noise energy = original band). {\f1 >1.0} = louder-than-original noise fill, {\f1 <1.0} = quieter. Directly scales the coded noise energy \u8212- this is the "how loud is the noise" knob. Decimal input. | 290|| {\f1 --pns-tonality <x>} | >=0.0 | -1 (off) | Scales the PNS tonality detection threshold. {\f1 1.0} = default; higher = more (even less-noisy) bands qualify as PNS = WIDER noise substitution. | 291|| {\f1 --pns-refpower <x>} | >=0.0 | -1 (off) | Scales the PNS reference-power detection threshold. {\f1 1.0} = default. | 292|| {\f1 --pns-gapfill <x>} | >=0.0 | -1 (off) | Scales the PNS gap-fill threshold (fills PNS holes between two PNS bands). {\f1 1.0} = default. Advanced/subtle \u8212- rarely visible. | 293|| {\f1 --pns-min-width <n>} | -1 off, >=1 | -1 | Minimum SFB width for PNS. Effective above the built-in default (LC=16); e.g. 32/64 restricts PNS to wider bands. | 294|| {\f1 --afterburner <n>} | 0/1 (also stock {\f1 -a}) | 1 | Afterburner (more precise quantization). | 295| 296|IMPORTANT about PNS at low bitrate: FDK has a tuning table ({\f1 levelTable}) that 297|COMPLETELY disables PNS below ~28 kbps (the bitrate row 0-27999 = all zeros for 298|every samplerate). That is why at 24 kbps {\f1 --pns}/{\f1 --pns-start} do NOTHING (the 299|audio sounds "like MP3/MDCT"), while at 64 kbps the difference is large. 300|{\f1 --force-pns} bypasses this gate (uses the first active row of the table), so PNS 301|also works at 24k. FDK limitation: PNS still requires TNS enabled and a non-VBR 302|mode \u8212- otherwise it is zeroed higher up in the chain (we can't do anything about 303|it without a deeper rebuild). 304| 305|## 6. Masking / ATH 306| 307|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 308||---|---|---|---| 309|| {\f1 --ath-scale <n>} | 1..~4096 (Q8) | 256 | Masking threshold scale in Q8 (256 = x1.0). {\f1 >256} raises the thresholds (more noise, fewer bits per band), {\f1 <256} lowers them (cleaner, more bits). Works in FDK's ld64 domain as an additive log2 offset. NOTE: this only touches the log-domain threshold copy and is partly undone downstream by the min-SNR / 29 dB clamps \u8212- for a stronger, more direct effect prefer {\f1 --minsnr-scale} below. | 310|| {\f1 --spread-mask <n>} | Q8, >=0 | -1 (off) | Scales the spreading of masking between bands. {\f1 <256} = less masking = more detail. Biggest effect where bits are limited (96-192k). | 311|| {\f1 --minsnr-scale <n>} | 1..no limit (Q8) | -1 (off) | MusePack-style: scales the REQUIRED per-band coding SNR ({\f1 sfbMinSnrLdData}, FDK's closest thing to TMN/NMT). {\f1 <256} = demand HIGHER SNR = more detail/bits; {\f1 >256} = coarser. More effective than {\f1 --ath-scale} because min-SNR is what the avoid-holes logic clamps thresholds back to. 256=off. | 312|| {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi <n>} | 1..no limit (Q8) | -1 (off) | Scales FDK's MAX_SNR ceiling (~\u8722 ?1 dB). {\f1 >256} lets bands demand more than the stock cap. 256=off. | 313|| {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo <n>} | 1..no limit (Q8) | -1 (off) | Scales FDK's MIN_SNR floor (~\u8722 ?25 dB). 256=off. | 314|| {\f1 --reduce-clamp <0\\|1>} | 0, 1 | 1 (on) | {\f1 0} drops the "29 dB Ratio" threshold-reduction ceiling in the CBR quantizer, letting thresholds be pushed deeper (more bits into demanding bands). Pairs with {\f1 --minsnr-scale} for extreme detail. CBR only (VBR uses a different path). | 315| 316|### What really helps at low and medium bitrate (10-144 kbps) 317| 318|A common question: can you still squeeze something out of coding 319|accuracy/efficiency (Huffman, quantization iterations, etc.)? The honest answer 320|after reviewing the FDK code: 321| 322|- Huffman coding (section merging, codebook selection in {\f1 dyn_bits.cpp}) is 323| already optimal (greedy section merging giving min. bits). There is no 324| meaningful knob there \u8212- and exposing it would only worsen the result. 325|- The quantization iteration loop ({\f1 maxIterations}) is a RESCUE mechanism for bit 326| shortage; increasing it does nothing (details in the manual, chapter 9a). 327|- Internal thresholds (minSnr adaptation, {\f1 bits2PeFactor}) are fixed-point 328| arithmetic with hard ranges \u8212- moving them risks instability, not improvement. 329| 330|The REAL set of quality levers for 10-144 kbps is ALREADY exposed: 331|- {\f1 --ath-scale <256} \u8212- globally lower the masking threshold (more detail for bits). 332|- {\f1 --spread-mask <256} \u8212- less inter-band masking (more bands coded). 333|- {\f1 --ms-precision >256} \u8212- shallower holes in the MS bands. 334|- {\f1 --is-aggression} \u8212- control intensity stereo (crucial at low bitrate). 335|- {\f1 --force-pns} + {\f1 --pns-start} \u8212- noise control at very low bitrate. 336|- under HE-AAC: {\f1 --sbr-invf}, {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset}, {\f1 --speech} (speech). 337| 338|This is not missing functionality \u8212- these are the same levers that professional 339|tuning uses, just manually. Start with {\f1 --ath-scale} and {\f1 --spread-mask}, one at 340|a time. 341| 342|## 7. Bias of short/long block switching (any profile) 343| 344|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 345||---|---|---|---| 346|| {\f1 --block-bias <n>} | 0..255 | -1 (off) | Shifts the short/long decision threshold. 128 = encoder default (no change), >128 favors short blocks (more "transient"), <128 favors long ones, 0 = practically only long. | 347| 348|IMPORTANT: {\f1 --block-bias} always produces a standard-compliant stream (it shifts 349|the attack-detection threshold, it doesn't forcibly impose a block type). It 350|replaced the old {\f1 --allshort}/{\f1 --alllong}, which created an ILLEGAL stream (hard 351|forcing of the short window without recomputing SFB/grouping -> decoders rejected 352|it, Winamp "skipped like on a scratched disc"). If you want maximum long blocks: 353|{\f1 --block-bias 0}; maximum short: {\f1 --block-bias 255}. 354| 355|## 8. MS decision bias (honestly: a tool with a WEAK effect) 356| 357|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 358||---|---|---|---| 359|| {\f1 --ms-bias <n>} | 0..255 (Q8) | -1 (off) | Shifts the L/R vs MS decision threshold. Q8, 128 = +0.5 in FDK's ld64 units. >0 = MS more eager. Reacts already from ~32 (after scale recalibration). | 360| 361|HONESTLY about {\f1 --ms-bias} \u8212- this is the weakest tool in the whole set, and now 362|we know why "it does little". MS (mid/side) is a LOSSLESS transform: mid=L+R, 363|side=L-R reconstructs exactly back to L/R. Enabling/disabling MS on a given band 364|does NOT change the sound \u8212- it only changes HOW MANY BITS the encoding takes. The 365|encoder chooses a near-optimal decision per band anyway; {\f1 --ms-bias} only shifts a 366|few BORDERLINE bands. Measurement (L/R correlation after decoding, ADTS size): 367|effect on the order of <0.1% of the size and correlation changes in the 4th 368|decimal place. 369| 370|Technical note: in the previous version the bias scale was ~256x too weak 371|(multiplier <<15 instead of <<23) \u8212- hence "128 did nothing, only 2048 moved 372|something". Now 128 = a real +0.5 ld64 as in the description, so it reacts from 373|~32. But even a correctly scaled bias inherently has a small impact (see above). 374| 375|Want to REALLY control MS? Use the hard switches, not the bias: 376|- {\f1 --msmask 0} \u8212- DISABLE MS entirely (pure, independent L/R). This is the right 377| choice for center-cancel / vocal removal (zero channel mixing by the coder). 378|- {\f1 --msmask 1} \u8212- force MS on all bands (max bit economy). 379|- {\f1 --msbands} / {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} \u8212- restrict MS to selected bands. 380|Measurement: msmask 0 vs 1 gives ~900 B difference on a 2s sample; ms-bias only ~2 B. 381| 382|## 9. Quasi-constrained VBR (CBR engine + wider breathing) 383| 384|IMPORTANT: without these switches, CBR is 100% UNCHANGED (verified: bit-identical 385|to the original binary). You enable them deliberately. 386| 387|How AAC breathes: even in CBR, frames borrow from the bit-reservoir, so one frame 388|can be ~122 kbps and the next ~141, as long as the average = target. These knobs 389|widen/narrow that breathing. 390| 391|HARD CEILING for everything: an AAC frame holds MAX 6144 bits per channel 392|(=768 bytes/channel); stereo => 12288 bits/frame. At 44100 Hz one frame = 393|1024 samples = ~23.2 ms, so bits/frame = kbps * 23.22. (E.g. 128k stereo: 394|average ~2972 bits/frame; ceiling 12288.) 395| 396|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 397||---|---|---|---| 398|| {\f1 --vbr-reservoir <bits>} | 0..(6144{\i channels - average) | -1 (off) | Bit-reservoir size. More = wider frame spread around the average. min 0 (stick to the target tightly). Auto-clamped to the ceiling - you can't overdo it. Safe start: 2-3x default. | 399|| {\f1 --peak-bitrate <bps>} | > target | -1 (off) | Allows short peaks up to this value while keeping the average. Set ABOVE {\f1 -b} (e.g. -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 160000). Below the target it is ignored. | 400|| {\f1 --max-bits-frame <bits>} | average..12288(st.) | -1 (off) | Hard ceiling of bits in ONE frame. Must be >= average and <= 6144}channels (otherwise clamped). Reasonable cap ~1.5x average (~4500 for 128k st.). Too low = starves loud frames (audible). | 401|| {\f1 --min-bits-frame <bits>} | 0..average | -1 (off) | Hard floor of bits/frame. A higher floor wastes bits on silence. Leave at 0 unless experimenting. | 402|| {\f1 --bitres-mode <n>} | 0/1/2 | -1 (def) | Reservoir mode: 0 full (like default), 1 reduced, 2 disabled (rigid, closest to hard per-frame CBR). | 403| 404|HOW TO SET IT OPTIMALLY (for the less experienced - so you don't overdo it): 405|- Safe quasi-CVBR ~128k stereo: {\f1 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 160000 --vbr-reservoir 6000}. 406|- Do NOT set {\f1 --max-bits-frame} BELOW the average or {\f1 --min-bits-frame} ABOVE the 407| average - that fights the target and ruins quality. 408|- {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} is auto-clamped to the ceiling, so it's safe to experiment. 409|- Measured for real (4s variable signal, 128k stereo): CBR default spread 95-167 410| kbps; with {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 8000 --peak-bitrate 192000} spread 36-158 kbps 411| (breathes harder, average held); {\f1 --bitres-mode 2} spread 127.8-128.2 412| (rigid). All fully decodable. 413|- Limitation: this is a CBR+reservoir engine, not true ABR like LAME. Swings are 414| moderate (6144 bits/channel limit), but this is that "light flight" of AAC. 415| 416|## 10. Audiophile / extreme (opt-in, beyond the typical range) 417| 418|| Switch | Values | Default | Description | 419||---|---|---|---| 420|| {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} | flag | off | Remove the hard 20 kHz core cap. {\f1 --core-cutoff} can then reach all the way to Nyquist. | 421|| {\f1 --is-aggression <0..100>} | 0..100 | -1 (off) | IS aggression slider (see section 1). | 422|| {\f1 --force-pns} | flag | off | PNS below the ~28 kbps gate (see section 5). | 423|| {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} | flag | off | Remove the LOWER bitrate threshold. Allows extremely low: 8k HE-AAC stereo, 6k LC. IMPORTANT: in this mode {\f1 -b} is taken LITERALLY as bps (without the nu774 x1000 convention), so {\f1 -b 6000} = 6000 bps. The upper ceiling 6144*channels stays (hard AAC limit). Residual floor ~10 kbps = the minimum of AAC headers. | 424|| {\f1 --speech} | flag | off | SBR tuning mode for human SPEECH (different inverse-filtering thresholds, noise level, no parametric coding). Applies to HE-AAC (SBR); LC has no separate speech mode. For pure speech at low bitrate. | 425|| {\f1 --spread-mask <n>} | Q8, >=0 | -1 (off) | Scales the spreading of masking between bands. {\f1 <256} = LESS masking = more detail (equivalent to loosening tone-masks-noise). Biggest effect where bits are limited (96-192k). 256=no change. Combine with {\f1 --ath-scale <256}. | 426| 427|BANDWIDTH ABOVE 20 kHz (audiophile): FDK has a hardcoded cap {\f1 min(20000, sr/2)} on 428|the core bandwidth \u8212- even with a 96 kHz input and high bitrate, nothing above 429|20 kHz is actually coded (your suspicion was correct). {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} lifts 430|this cap; then {\f1 --core-cutoff} controls the bandwidth all the way up to sr/2. 431| 432|Measured (96 kHz input, LC 400k, broadband noise): 433|- without uncap, {\f1 --core-cutoff 40000}: verbose 20000 Hz, energy >20 kHz ~= 0%. 434|- {\f1 --core-cutoff 40000 --uncap-bandwidth}: verbose 40000 Hz, energy 20-24 kHz 435| ~10%, 24-32 kHz ~20%, 32-44 kHz ~20% \u8212- full band up to 40 kHz actually coded. 436| 437|Audiophile preset (full bandwidth + manual masking, for 190+ kbps): 438|{\f1  439|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 \\ 440|    --ath-scale 200 -o out.m4a in96k.wav 441|} 442|{\f1 --ath-scale <256} lowers the masking thresholds (cleaner, more bits for detail) \u8212- 443|sensible when you have a large bitrate margin. Note: bandwidth >20 kHz and extreme 444|settings may be rejected by some decoders (outside the typical spec) \u8212- a conscious 445|opt-in. 446| 447|--- 448| 449|## 11. MP4/M4A container \u8212- which boxes are necessary, and what can be cut 450| 451|An .m4a file is a set of nested "boxes" (atoms). Verified what is what: 452| 453|MANDATORY (without them the file is UNPLAYABLE \u8212- there are no switches for them): 454|{\f1 ftyp}, {\f1 mdat} (raw audio data), and the skeleton {\f1 moov} \u8594 ? {\f1 mvhd} + {\f1 trak} \u8594 ? 455|{\f1 tkhd} \u8594 ? {\f1 mdia} ({\f1 mdhd}/{\f1 hdlr}/{\f1 minf} \u8594 ? {\f1 smhd}/{\f1 dinf}/{\f1 stbl} with the tables 456|{\f1 stsd}/{\f1 stts}/{\f1 stsc}/{\f1 stsz}/{\f1 stco}). This is the minimal file map required by the 457|ISO standard \u8212- every decoder needs it to even find and play the sound. Cutting 458|them makes no sense (result: a corrupt file). 459| 460|OPTIONAL (can be disabled) \u8212- the entire block {\f1 udta} \u8594 ? {\f1 meta} \u8594 ? {\f1 ilst}, i.e.: 461|- the encoder identification tag ({\f1 \u169 ?too}, now "PompAAC based on \u8230 ?"), 462|- {\f1 iTunSMPB} \u8212- encoder delay data for seamless (gapless) playback, 463|- all tags (title, artist, album, etc.). 464| 465|| Switch | Works on | Description | 466||---|---|---| 467|| {\f1 --no-tool-tag} | .m4a | Do not write the encoder identification tag ({\f1 \u169 ?too}). The rest of the tags and gapless stay. | 468|| {\f1 --minimal-moov} | .m4a | The smallest legal .m4a: omits the ENTIRE {\f1 udta}/metadata block (encoder tag + gapless iTunSMPB + all tags). The playback skeleton stays intact. | 469| 470|How much it saves (2 s, 128k stereo, measured): default ~34381 B \u8594 ? 471|{\f1 --no-tool-tag} ~34297 B (\u8722 ?84) \u8594 ? {\f1 --minimal-moov} ~34048 B (\u8722 ?333). These are small 472|numbers \u8212- the MP4 overhead is mostly the mandatory skeleton, which can't be 473|removed. Want truly zero container overhead? Use raw ADTS: {\f1 -f 2 -o out.aac} 474|(a stream without any boxes, but also without tags and gapless). 475| 476|NOTE on gapless: {\f1 --minimal-moov} removes iTunSMPB, so when joining tracks 477|micro-gaps may appear (the encoder delay won't be signaled). For ordinary 478|listening this doesn't matter; for "gapless" albums leave the defaults. 479| 480|--- 481| 482|## 12. Legend for reading {\f1 --verbose} 483| 484|{\f1 --verbose} prints RAW values (without hints in parentheses, so as not to clutter). 485|Below is what the non-obvious ones mean: 486| 487|| Field | How to read it | 488||---|---| 489|| {\f1 AOT (profile)} | 2=AAC-LC, 5=HE-AAC, 29=HE-AAC v2, 23=AAC-LD, 39=AAC-ELD. | 490|| {\f1 bitrate-mode} | 0=CBR, 1..5=VBR (higher=better). | 491|| {\f1 channel-mode} | 1=mono, 2=stereo (for HE-AAC v2 the core is mono, stereo is done by PS). | 492|| {\f1 core bandwidth} | Upper frequency of the AAC core, {\b anchored to the nearest SFB boundary} (the real cutoff, which can differ from the {\f1 -w}/{\f1 --core-cutoff} value you typed, e.g. {\f1 -w 17300} \u8594 ? {\f1 17915 Hz (SFB-anchored)}). In parentheses the SOURCE: {\f1 from -w}, {\f1 from --core-cutoff}, or {\f1 auto}. Under SBR this is only the core \u8212- SBR plays higher. | 493|| {\f1 final BW (AAC+SBR)} | Shown only when SBR is active: approximate UPPER frequency of the whole signal (core + SBR), computed from the {\f1 sbr stop freq index}. This is the equivalent of {\f1 core bandwidth}, but for the full HE-AAC band. | 494|| {\f1 signaling-mode} | Way of signaling SBR/PS: 0=implicit, 1=explicit backward-compat, 2=explicit hierarchical, auto=the library chooses. | 495|| {\f1 SBR mode} | Internal SBR mode (-1/0 when unused). | 496|| {\f1 sbr-ratio} | 1=downsampled (single-rate), 2=dual-rate (core at half the frequency). | 497|| {\f1 sbr amp res} | 0=1.5 dB, 1=3.0 dB (envelope amplitude resolution). | 498|| {\f1 granule/frame length} | Frame length in samples (1024 for LC, 512/480 for LD/ELD). | 499|| {\f1 codec delay} | Codec delay in samples/channel (total and the core alone). For gapless. | 500|| {\f1 IS corr threshold} / {\f1 IS L/R ratio} | Thresholds on the Q8 scale: 256 = 1.0. A lower correlation threshold = intensity stereo MORE eager (counterintuitively). | 501|| {\f1 IS min contiguous SFBs} | How many adjacent bands must "agree" before IS turns on. | 502|| {\f1 TNS mask} | Bitmask 0x0..0xF of which TNS filters are active. | 503|| {\f1 MS/IS bands: auto up to N} | Upper band index up to which the tool may operate. | 504|| {\f1 franken overrides applied} | List of switches that in THIS run deviate from pure FDK (or "none"). | 505| 506|Q8 values (like {\f1 IS corr threshold}, {\f1 --ms-precision}, {\f1 --ms-bias}, 507|{\f1 --ath-scale}, {\f1 --spread-mask}) are fixed-point numbers where 256 = 1.0; 508|e.g. 243 means 243/256 \u8776 ? 0.95. 509| 510|--- 511| 512|## 13. Reference tables (from the FDK tuning tables) 513| 514|Three orientation tables, so you can consciously choose {\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --sbr-start/stop} 515|and {\f1 -w}. The values are computed from the tables in the FDK code; they are 516|APPROXIMATE (the SFB grid is stepped), but they show the right order of magnitude. 517| 518|### Table 1 \u8212- approximate upper band frequency (SFB) [Hz] 519| 520|Bands numbered from the bottom (0=bass). Shown every 4th band; the last row = the 521|number of bands and the Nyquist frequency. Useful for {\f1 --msbands}/{\f1 --isbands}/{\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi}. 522| 523|| SFB | 16 kHz | 22.05 kHz | 32 kHz | 44.1 kHz | 48 kHz | 96 kHz | 524||----:|-------:|----------:|-------:|---------:|-------:|-------:| 525|| 0 | 62 | 43 | 62 | 86 | 94 | 188 | 526|| 4 | 312 | 215 | 312 | 431 | 469 | 938 | 527|| 8 | 562 | 388 | 562 | 775 | 844 | 1688 | 528|| 12 | 875 | 646 | 1000 | 1378 | 1500 | 2438 | 529|| 16 | 1250 | 991 | 1500 | 2067 | 2250 | 3750 | 530|| 20 | 1656 | 1335 | 2250 | 3101 | 3375 | 5625 | 531|| 24 | 2188 | 1852 | 3375 | 4651 | 5062 | 8062 | 532|| 28 | 2875 | 2584 | 5000 | 6891 | 7500 | 12938 | 533|| 32 | 3844 | 3618 | 7000 | 9647 | 10500 | 24000 | 534|| 36 | 5188 | 5039 | 9000 | 12403 | 13500 | 36000 | 535|| 40 | 7000 | 7020 | 11000| 15159 | 16500 | 48000 | 536|| 44 | \u8212- | 9647 | 13000| 17916 | 19500 | \u8212- | 537|| 48 | \u8212- | \u8212- | 15000| 22050 | 24000 | \u8212- | 538|| {\b number of bands / Nyquist} | 43 / 8000 | 47 / 11025 | 51 / 16000 | 49 / 22050 | 49 / 24000 | 41 / 48000 | 539| 540|### Table 2 \u8212- SBR: start freq index \u8594 ? approximate crossover frequency [Hz] 541| 542|This is the frequency from which SBR takes over the band above the AAC core 543|({\f1 --sbr-start}, index 0..15; lower = SBR starts lower = narrower core). "core" is 544|the core frequency; with dual-rate the output is twice as high (e.g. core 24k \u8594 ? 545|output 48k). 546| 547|| start index | core 16 kHz | core 24 kHz | core 32 kHz | core 44.1 kHz | core 48 kHz | 548||----:|----:|----:|----:|----:|----:| 549|| 0 | 2750 | 2250 | 2500 | 1378 | 1500 | 550|| 1 | 3000 | 2625 | 3000 | 2067 | 2250 | 551|| 2 | 3250 | 3000 | 3500 | 2756 | 3000 | 552|| 3 | 3500 | 3375 | 4000 | 3445 | 3750 | 553|| 4 | 3750 | 3750 | 4500 | 4134 | 4500 | 554|| 5 | 4000 | 4125 | 5000 | 4823 | 5250 | 555|| 6 | 4250 | 4500 | 5500 | 5512 | 6000 | 556|| 7 | 4500 | 4875 | 6000 | 6202 | 6750 | 557|| 8 | 4750 | 5250 | 6500 | 6891 | 7500 | 558| 559|Stop freq ({\f1 --sbr-stop}, 0..13) works analogously on the upper SBR boundary \u8212- 560|a higher index = SBR reaches higher (closer to the output Nyquist). By default the 561|library chooses both according to bitrate. 562| 563|### Table 3 \u8212- AAC-LC: approximate cutoff ({\f1 -w}/auto) by bitrate per channel [Hz] 564| 565|When you don't provide {\f1 -w}, FDK picks the bandwidth from this table according to 566|bitrate PER CHANNEL (stereo 128k = 64k/channel). The values are interpolated; the 567|mono and stereo columns differ. Helps assess whether {\f1 -w} makes sense (providing a 568|higher value than auto has an effect only if there are spare bits). 569| 570|| bitrate/channel | bandwidth (mono) | bandwidth (stereo+) | 571||----:|----:|----:| 572|| 0\u8211-12 kbps | 3700 | 5000 | 573|| 20 kbps | 6900 | 9640 | 574|| 28 kbps | 9600 | 13050 | 575|| 40 kbps | 12060 | 14260 | 576|| 56 kbps | 13950 | 15500 | 577|| 72 kbps | 14200 | 16120 | 578|| \u8805 ?96 kbps | 17000 | 17000 | 579| 580|Note: this table is SHARED for 32/44.1/48 kHz and higher \u8212- FDK indexes it by 581|bitrate per channel, not samplerate (samplerate only affects the upper limit = 582|Nyquist). For LC without SBR the real ceiling is ~17 kHz with auto; higher only 583|via {\f1 -w} (with spare bits) or {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} at sr\u8805 ?96k. 584| 585|--- 586| 587|## 14. DAB+ output ({\f1 --dab}, {\f1 --dab-label}) 588| 589|A dedicated output mode that emits a DAB+ digital-radio stream instead of an 590|MP4/M4A file or a bare ADTS stream. The encoder produces the AAC audio exactly as 591|the DAB+ system expects it (960-sample transform, 120 ms super frame, error 592|protection), so the stream can be handed straight to a multiplexer such as 593|{\f1 odr-dabmux} \u8594 ? ETI \u8594 ? transmitter (or a soft receiver like welle.io / dablin). 594| 595|DAB+ is not "AAC in a different box": it uses the 960-sample MDCT (not 1024), packs 596|audio into 120 ms {\b super frames}, guards the header with a {\b firecode} (Fire 597|CRC), and protects the payload with {\b Reed-Solomon RS(120,110)} over GF(256). The 598|output is a raw {\f1 .dabp} stream \u8212- successive super frames back to back \u8212- which is 599|what a DAB+ multiplexer ingests. 600| 601|### {\f1 --dab} 602|Turn on DAB+ super-frame output. The result is a raw {\f1 .dabp} stream (no MP4, no 603|ADTS). Constraints imposed by the standard: 604| 605|| Requirement | Value | 606||---|---| 607|| Sample rate | MUST be {\f1 32000} or {\f1 48000} Hz | 608|| Bitrate | multiple of 8 kbps, range 8..192 kbps | 609|| Channels | mono or stereo | 610|| Profiles | AAC-LC, HE-AAC, HE-AAC v2 (all three) | 611| 612|The profile (AOT) is picked AUTOMATICALLY from bitrate and channel count, the same 613|way {\f1 odr-audioenc} does it \u8212- you normally don't set {\f1 -p}: 614| 615|- stereo \u8804 ?48 kbps (subchannel \u8804 ?6) \u8594 ? {\b HE-AAC v2} (PS), 616|- mono \u8804 ?64k or stereo \u8804 ?80k \u8594 ? {\b HE-AAC} (SBR), 617|- higher \u8594 ? {\b AAC-LC}. 618| 619|You can still force the profile with {\f1 -p} ({\f1 2}=LC, {\f1 5}=HE-AAC, {\f1 29}=HE-AAC v2) if 620|you know what you want. 621| 622|### {\f1 --dab-label <text>} 623|A static {\b DLS} (Dynamic Label Segment) carried as X-PAD inside the super frame; 624|DAB+ receivers show it as the station name / title. Up to ~48 characters (three 625|segments in one PAD). "Static" means one fixed string for the whole file \u8212- 626|time-varying labels and MOT slideshow (the {\f1 ODR-PadEnc} model) are planned for 627|later. Without {\f1 --dab} the label is ignored. 628| 629|### Examples 630| 631|{\f1  632|# 48k/32k stereo, ~96k \u8594 ? auto AAC-LC: 633|fdkaac --dab -b 96 -o out.dabp input.wav 634| 635|# \u8594 ? auto HE-AAC (SBR): 636|fdkaac --dab -b 64 -o out.dabp input.wav 637| 638|# \u8594 ? auto HE-AAC v2 (PS): 639|fdkaac --dab -b 32 -o out.dabp input.wav 640| 641|# with a station label: 642|fdkaac --dab -b 96 --dab-label "Radio DHT" -o out.dabp input.wav 643| 644|# broadcast chain: 645|# out.dabp \u8594 ? odr-dabmux \u8594 ? ETI \u8594 ? transmitter / decoder 646|} 647| 648|Note: without {\f1 --dab} the encoder behaves exactly as before \u8212- zero impact, 649|bit-identical to stock. Verified: the streams decode with an independent faad2 650|decoder (dablin), the DLS label is readable, and the LC output is bit-identical to 651|the reference {\f1 odr-audioenc}. Nine combinations (48/32 kHz \u215 ? mono/stereo \u215 ? the 652|three profiles) were tested, each independently decodable. 653| 654|--- 655| 656|## Examples 657| 658|{\f1  659|# Starting point \u8212- what the encoder set: 660|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --verbose -o out.m4a in.wav 661| 662|# Quasi-constrained VBR ~128k (safe preset): 663|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 160000 --vbr-reservoir 6000 -o out.m4a in.wav 664| 665|# Aggressive intensity stereo at 64k: 666|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 64000 --is 1 --is-aggression 70 -o out.m4a in.wav 667| 668|# Force PNS at 24k (otherwise the FDK gate disables it): 669|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 24000 --pns 1 --force-pns -o out.m4a in.wav 670| 671|# Audiophile full 40 kHz band from a 96 kHz input: 672|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 -o out.m4a in96k.wav 673| 674|# MS on the 5 highest bands + shallower holes (not the lowest): 675|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48 --ms-precision 448 -o out.m4a in.wav 676| 677|# Extremely low HE-AAC v2: 8000 bps stereo 48 kHz: 678|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate -o out.m4a in48k.wav 679| 680|# SBR: full stereo separation LR + forced inverse filtering: 681|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 96000 --sbr-stereo-mode 1 --sbr-invf 2 -o out.m4a in.wav 682| 683|# Your case: 7.5 kHz of core under HE-AAC v2 48k: 684|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --core-cutoff 7500 -o out.m4a in.wav 685| 686|# Completely independent stereo (no MS/IS) on LC 128k: 687|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --msmask 0 --is 0 -o out.m4a in.wav 688| 689|# MS only on the 6 lower bands: 690|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --msmask 1 --msbands 6 -o out.m4a in.wav 691| 692|# Only short blocks + limited TNS: 693|# Only long blocks (bias) + limited TNS: 694|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --block-bias 0 --tns-order 2 -o out.m4a in.wav 695| 696|# More aggressive masking (thresholds x2) + earlier PNS: 697|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 80000 --ath-scale 512 --pns 1 --pns-start 4000 -o out.m4a in.wav 698| 699|# Denser SBR noise description + more precise amplitude: 700|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-noise-bands 5 --sbr-amp-res 0 -o out.m4a in.wav 701| 702|# HE-AAC v2 with PS disabled (flattened stereo): 703|fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 32000 --ps 0 -o out.m4a in.wav 704|} 705| 706|--- 707| 708|## Default quality vs the original binary (fdkaac2.exe) 709| 710|Verification (2026-07-21) whether franken's default settings = the original 711|supplied binary (dBpoweramp R17, the same libfdk-aac version 4.0.1 / package 2.0.3): 712|- AAC-LC: {\b bit-identical} (the same md5) \u8212- zero regressions. 713|- HE-AAC v1/v2: the bytes differ, BUT: the HE-AAC v2 spectrum is identical to 714| 0.000 dB, and the HE-AAC v1 error relative to the original is practically the 715| same as in the original (7.05e11 vs 7.05e11). The byte difference stems from a 716| different compiler (mingw/gcc vs MSVC) and fixed-point rounding, NOT from worse 717| quality. The audible SBR "otherness" is a different, equally correct 718| implementation, not a quality loss. 719| 720|--- 721| 722|## Tests (make check) 723| 724|Upstream fdk-aac/nu774 have no unit tests ({\f1 make check} in them = a no-op). This 725|project has its OWN functional test suite in {\f1 tests/check.sh}, run with: 726| 727|{\f1  728|make check 729|} 730| 731|It checks (on the x64 + x86 binaries if present): completeness of the switches in 732|--help, decodability of the stream for each switch (ffmpeg, 0 errors), real 733|operation of quasi-CVBR (CVBR breathes wider than the rigid bitres-mode2), verbose 734|without -1 values, and NO REGRESSION (default CBR without franken flags = ADTS 735|bit-identical to the original fdkaac2.exe). Requires ffmpeg + python3 (present in 736|WSL). Exit 0 = OK, 1 = errors, 77 = missing dependencies. Last result: 27/27 PASS. 737| 738|--- 739| 740|The sources with the applied patches live in {\f1 src-fdk-aac/} and {\f1 src-fdkaac/}. 741|All changes in libfdk are wired through a single module 742|{\f1 libAACenc/src/franken.\{h,cpp\}} (the global {\f1 g_franken} block, sentinels = FDK 743|defaults), and the new {\f1 AACENC_PARAM}s (range {\f1 0xF0xx}) are exposed in 744|{\f1 aacenc_lib.h}. 745| 746|{\f1 bash 747|sudo apt-get install -y mingw-w64          # + autotools (autoconf/automake/libtool) 748| 749|# --- libfdk-aac (x64) --- 750|cd src-fdk-aac && autoreconf -i 751|./configure --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 --prefix=$PWD/../inst-x64 \\ 752|    --enable-static --disable-shared CFLAGS=-O2 CXXFLAGS=-O2 753|make -j && make install 754| 755|# --- frontend (x64) --- 756|cd ../src-fdkaac && autoreconf -i 757|PKG_CONFIG_PATH=$PWD/../inst-x64/lib/pkgconfig ./configure \\ 758|    --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 CFLAGS="-O2 -I$PWD/../inst-x64/include" \\ 759|    LDFLAGS="-static -static-libgcc -L$PWD/../inst-x64/lib" 760|make -j     # -> fdkaac.exe 761| 762|# x86: the same with --host=i686-w64-mingw32 and a separate inst-x86 prefix. 763|} 764| 765|## List of changed FDK files (mapped to points) 766|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/franken.\{h,cpp\}} \u8212- new control module. 767|- {\f1 libAACenc/include/aacenc_lib.h} \u8212- new {\f1 AACENC_PARAM} 0xF0xx. 768|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/aacenc_lib.cpp} \u8212- SetParam dispatch + override of useMS/IS/PNS/ 769| afterburner/cutoff + cutoff guard under SBR (after {\f1 sbrEncoder_Init}) + read-only 770| GetParam mirrors for verbose (useTns/Pns/IS/MS, effective SBR). 771|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/ms_stereo.cpp} \u8212- per-band MS control IN THE DECISION LOOP (mask 772| + L/R->M/S butterfly synchronized; fixed the "left=center" artifact) + MS bias 773| + MS band range (--msbands-lo/-hi) + MS precision (--ms-precision, ld64 threshold). 774|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/intensity.cpp} \u8212- IS band cap (consistent with the mask) + IS 775| threshold bias (initIsParams: min_is_sfbs, corr_thresh, left_right_ratio) + --is-aggression. 776|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/psy_configuration.cpp} \u8212- read-back of the SFB count + removal of the IS gate. 777|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/bandwidth.cpp} \u8212- --uncap-bandwidth (removing the 20kHz cap). 778|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/pnsparam.cpp} \u8212- PNS start override + --force-pns (bypass the table gate). 779|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/aacenc.cpp} \u8212- TNS mask override + quasi-CVBR + --unlock-bitrate 780| (removal of the lower bitrate floor in FDKaacEnc_LimitBitrate). 781|- {\f1 libSBRenc/src/sbr_encoder.cpp} \u8212- SBR density override + --sbr-num-env (static 782| framing) + --sbr-freqres-fixfix + --sbr-stereo-mode + --sbr-noise-floor-offset. 783|- {\f1 libSBRenc/src/invf_est.cpp} \u8212- --sbr-invf (forced inverse filtering level). 784|- {\f1 libSBRenc/src/ps_encode.cpp} \u8212- PS IID override + --ps-icc/--ps-icc-mode. 785|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/aacenc_tns.cpp} \u8212- TNS order cap. 786|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/pnsparam.cpp} \u8212- PNS start frequency override. 787|- {\f1 libSBRenc/src/sbr_encoder.cpp} \u8212- SBR density/precision override + writing the 788| effective SBR values to g_franken (for verbose). 789|- {\f1 libSBRenc/src/ps_encode.cpp} \u8212- PS override (forcing IID + quantization mode). 790|- {\f1 libAACenc/src/main.c}, {\f1 aacenc.c}, {\f1 aacenc.h} (frontend) \u8212- CLI switches, 791| parsing, passing to SetParam, {\f1 --verbose} dump, help. 792|\par}
